<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=periodic-table">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Periodic table</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1869">1869</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Periodic table of the elements</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The periodic table is an ordered arrangement of the chemical elements into rows (periods) and columns (groups), in which elements of the same group tend to show similar chemical properties; the first to become generally accepted was that of the Russian chemist Dmitri Mendeleev, published in 1869. He left gaps for elements not yet known and predicted the properties of three of them; gallium, scandium and germanium, discovered between 1875 and 1886, matched his predictions. The periodic law it depicts was explained early in the 20th century by the discovery of atomic numbers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/periodic-table.jpg" alt="Periodic table" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dmitri Mendeleev · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1869-periodic-table.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1869</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Russia</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Dmitri Mendeleev · <a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Lothar Meyer’s book of 1864 and John Newlands’s work of 1863–1864 held earlier versions; Mendeleev’s was the first to be generally accepted.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/atomic-theory">Atomic theory<span>1804–1808</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=periodic-table">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
